## Part 2: Start a fresh portfolio project

As in past weeks, open your browser and navigate to our template repository at <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/template-repo-homepage.png"
  alt="GitHub template repository page showing the Use this template button"
  chromeTitle="cuny-jour-static-site-template"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template"
/>

Click "Use this template" and create a new repository called `riis-portfolio`. Clone it. Open it in Visual Studio Code.

Install the dependencies.

```bash
npm install
```

Start the dev server.

```bash
npm run dev
```

Open [localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173). You'll see the article-style demo page our template ships with — the same one we've been recycling all semester.

A portfolio is not an article. It's something else entirely. So before we start adding new things, we need to clear away the old.

Open `src/routes/+page.svelte`. Delete every component used in the article body — `ArticleHeader`, `ArticleBody`, `Image`, `Blockquote`, `BigNumber`, anything else the template inlines as demo content. Remove their imports from the script block at the top. Leave the file with just an empty script block and an empty body.

Your homepage should now look something like this:

```svelte
<script>
</script>
```

That's the right starting point. Save and check your browser. The page will be blank. We're going to fill it with something better.

## Part 3: Meet the components

A portfolio homepage has two parts that matter: who you are, and what you've made. As in past weeks, your class template already includes the components we'll use. Like the rest of the library, they're documented on the [Storybook](https://palewire.github.io/cuny-jour-static-site-template/storybook/) site.

